export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"modelcraft","addon":"flow","name":"Material flow","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"topics":[{"slug":"flow-start","scope":"addon:flow","title":"Material flow: getting started","summary":"What the Material flow add-on does in ModelCraft, how to switch it on, and a first run with the example line.","section":"start","order":1,"keywords":["material flow","material handling","conveyor","simulation","throughput","warehouse","distribution center","agv","add-on"],"headings":[{"id":"what-it-is","text":"What it is","level":2},{"id":"a-first-run","text":"A first run","level":2},{"id":"where-the-layout-is-kept","text":"Where the layout is kept","level":2},{"id":"what-it-is-not","text":"What it is not","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-is\"\u003EWhat it is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMaterial flow is an add-on for ModelCraft that comes with it and is free. You lay out a material-handling system in the 3D view — conveyors, curves, inclines, zone conveyors, merges, diverts, stop gates, photo-eyes, stations, buffers and AGV paths — and run a simulation of it: loads arrive, travel, queue, get worked on and leave, and the add-on tells you the throughput, how long loads take, how busy each station is and where queues build up. You can play the run back in the 3D view.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt is off until you switch it on: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd-ons\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterial flow\u003C/span\u003E. Its tools are in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterial flow\u003C/span\u003E section of the right panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-first-run\"\u003EA first run\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExample line\u003C/span\u003E. A source, a conveyor, a divert, two lanes with a station each, a merge and a sink appear in the 3D view, and the view zooms to them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun\u003C/span\u003E. Eight hours are simulated; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EResults\u003C/span\u003E shows loads finished, loads per hour and lead times.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlay\u003C/span\u003E and choose a speed (60× shows a minute every second). Loads move along the belts, wait in front of the stations and take turns at the merge.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a station under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EResults\u003C/span\u003E to see its utilization and queue. Press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave results as CSV\u003C/span\u003E for a spreadsheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChange something — a station&#039;s time, a belt&#039;s speed, the divert&#039;s rule — and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun\u003C/span\u003E again to compare.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-the-layout-is-kept\"\u003EWhere the layout is kept\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe layout is kept in this browser. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave…\u003C/span\u003E writes it to a \u003Ccode\u003E.ccflow.json\u003C/code\u003E file and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen…\u003C/span\u003E reads one back; share that file to share a layout. It is not saved inside the \u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E file yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-is-not\"\u003EWhat it is not\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELoads are moved by rules, not by physics: they never tip, slide or fall, and they keep their place on a belt. The simulation is a planning tool: it shows what a layout can carry and where it backs up. Connecting a real PLC to the model is planned for CadCraft Studio later; the web edition simulates only.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What it is Material flow is an add-on for ModelCraft that comes with it and is free. You lay out a material-handling system in the 3D view — conveyors, curves, inclines, zone conveyors, merges, diverts, stop gates, photo-eyes, stations, buffers and AGV paths — and run a simulation of it: loads arrive, travel, queue, get worked on and leave, and the add-on tells you the throughput, how long loads take, how busy each station is and where queues build up. You can play the run back in the 3D view. It is off until you switch it on: Settings › General › Add-ons › Material flow. Its tools are in the Material flow section of the right panel. A first run Press Example line. A source, a conveyor, a divert, two lanes with a station each, a merge and a sink appear in the 3D view, and the view zooms to them. Press Run. Eight hours are simulated; Results shows loads finished, loads per hour and lead times. Press Play and choose a speed (60× shows a minute every second). Loads move along the belts, wait in front of the stations and take turns at the merge. Open a station under Results to see its utilization and queue. Press Save results as CSV for a spreadsheet. Change something — a station's time, a belt's speed, the divert's rule — and press Run again to compare. Where the layout is kept The layout is kept in this browser. Save… writes it to a .ccflow.json file and Open… reads one back; share that file to share a layout. It is not saved inside the .modelcraft file yet. What it is not Loads are moved by rules, not by physics: they never tip, slide or fall, and they keep their place on a belt. The simulation is a planning tool: it shows what a layout can carry and where it backs up. Connecting a real PLC to the model is planned for CadCraft Studio later; the web edition simulates only."},{"slug":"flow-layout","scope":"addon:flow","title":"Material flow: lay out a system","summary":"Add items from the catalog, place and move them with the mouse, join them port to port, and set their parameters.","section":"tasks","order":10,"keywords":["layout","catalog","port","snap","conveyor","curve","incline","merge","divert","gate","photo-eye","station","buffer","source","sink","agv path"],"headings":[{"id":"ports","text":"Ports","level":2},{"id":"add-items","text":"Add items","level":2},{"id":"place-and-move-with-the-mouse","text":"Place and move with the mouse","level":2},{"id":"the-catalog","text":"The catalog","level":2},{"id":"times","text":"Times","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"ports\"\u003EPorts\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery item has ports: an input where loads come in and one or more outputs where they leave, each with a direction. Two items are joined when an output and an input touch and point the same way. Joints are never stored: they are worked out from where things are, so moving an item and snapping it back keeps the flow right. Free ports show as small red markers in the 3D view.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-items\"\u003EAdd items\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd from the catalog\u003C/span\u003E, choose the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItem\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin to\u003C/span\u003E, choose the free output it should follow (the selected item&#039;s output is chosen for you). The new item is placed with its input on that output, facing the same way.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd\u003C/span\u003E. It is selected, and its parameters show below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-and-move-with-the-mouse\"\u003EPlace and move with the mouse\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace with mouse\u003C/span\u003E and click in the 3D view: the chosen item lands on the ground point you clicked and, when a free port is within 0.6 m, joins it. Keep clicking to place more; press Esc (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop placing\u003C/span\u003E) to stop.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag any item to move it: it slides over the ground at its own height and snaps onto a free port within 0.6 m when you let go. \u003Cstrong\u003EHold Shift\u003C/strong\u003E to drag it together with everything joined to it (belts, ports, AGV paths and the photo-eyes on them), so a whole line moves as one. Untick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove and place with the mouse\u003C/span\u003E to leave the 3D view to ModelCraft&#039;s own tools. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E step back through the changes you made here (the last 60).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe layout is saved in the \u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E file with the model, and opens with it. Where the add-on is off, or not installed, the file still keeps the layout untouched.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENothing (place it free)\u003C/span\u003E to place an item on its own; then set its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EY\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHeight\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETurn\u003C/span\u003E and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESnap\u003C/span\u003E: it jumps onto the nearest free port within 0.6 m.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-catalog\"\u003EThe catalog\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EItem\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConveyor, curve, incline\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA belt. Loads move at its speed and keep the gap you set. With \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAccumulate\u003C/span\u003E on, loads queue up nose to tail when the end is blocked; off, the whole belt stops. A curve turns left for a positive angle.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZone conveyor\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn accumulating belt in zones of equal length: one load a zone.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMerge\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo or three inputs, one output. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPriority\u003C/span\u003E lets input 1 go first; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFirst come\u003C/span\u003E the load that has waited longest; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETake turns\u003C/span\u003E alternates.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDivert\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne input, two or three outputs (straight on, left, right). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERound-robin\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFirst free\u003C/span\u003E (an output that can take the load now, in order), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy load type\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EA 1, B 2\u003C/code\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy share\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003E1 3\u003C/code\u003E sends a quarter to output 1) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy photo-eye\u003C/span\u003E (a lane whose photo-eye has a load standing on it counts as full).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETransfer\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHands a load across in a set time, one at a time.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStop gate\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHolds loads: open, closed, or open and closed in turn.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStation\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWorks on loads: servers, a queue in front, and the time each load takes. A load that is done but cannot leave keeps its server (blocked).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBuffer\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHolds a number of loads, first in, first out.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESource\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMakes loads: a time between loads, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlways one waiting\u003C/span\u003E to find the most a layout can carry. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELoad types\u003C/span\u003E is a list of type, share and length (\u003Ccode\u003EA 3 0.4, B 1 0.6\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESink\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhere loads leave and are counted.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPhoto-eye\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESits on a conveyor at a distance along it. It counts loads and tells a divert when a load stands on it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAGV path, pickup, drop\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESee &quot;Material flow: AGVs&quot;.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"times\"\u003ETimes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA time can be fixed or follow a distribution: uniform (least and most), exponential (mean: random arrivals), normal (mean and standard deviation, never below zero), triangular (least, most likely, most) or Erlang (mean and phases). All times are in seconds, lengths in meters.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Ports Every item has ports: an input where loads come in and one or more outputs where they leave, each with a direction. Two items are joined when an output and an input touch and point the same way. Joints are never stored: they are worked out from where things are, so moving an item and snapping it back keeps the flow right. Free ports show as small red markers in the 3D view. Add items Under Add from the catalog, choose the Item. In Join to, choose the free output it should follow (the selected item's output is chosen for you). The new item is placed with its input on that output, facing the same way. Press Add. It is selected, and its parameters show below. Place and move with the mouse Press Place with mouse and click in the 3D view: the chosen item lands on the ground point you clicked and, when a free port is within 0.6 m, joins it. Keep clicking to place more; press Esc (or Stop placing) to stop. Drag any item to move it: it slides over the ground at its own height and snaps onto a free port within 0.6 m when you let go. Hold Shift to drag it together with everything joined to it (belts, ports, AGV paths and the photo-eyes on them), so a whole line moves as one. Untick Move and place with the mouse to leave the 3D view to ModelCraft's own tools. Undo and Redo step back through the changes you made here (the last 60). The layout is saved in the .modelcraft file with the model, and opens with it. Where the add-on is off, or not installed, the file still keeps the layout untouched. Choose Nothing (place it free) to place an item on its own; then set its X, Y, Height and Turn and press Snap: it jumps onto the nearest free port within 0.6 m. The catalog Item What it does Conveyor, curve, incline A belt. Loads move at its speed and keep the gap you set. With Accumulate on, loads queue up nose to tail when the end is blocked; off, the whole belt stops. A curve turns left for a positive angle. Zone conveyor An accumulating belt in zones of equal length: one load a zone. Merge Two or three inputs, one output. Priority lets input 1 go first; First come the load that has waited longest; Take turns alternates. Divert One input, two or three outputs (straight on, left, right). Round-robin, First free (an output that can take the load now, in order), By load type (A 1, B 2), By share (1 3 sends a quarter to output 1) or By photo-eye (a lane whose photo-eye has a load standing on it counts as full). Transfer Hands a load across in a set time, one at a time. Stop gate Holds loads: open, closed, or open and closed in turn. Station Works on loads: servers, a queue in front, and the time each load takes. A load that is done but cannot leave keeps its server (blocked). Buffer Holds a number of loads, first in, first out. Source Makes loads: a time between loads, or Always one waiting to find the most a layout can carry. Load types is a list of type, share and length (A 3 0.4, B 1 0.6). Sink Where loads leave and are counted. Photo-eye Sits on a conveyor at a distance along it. It counts loads and tells a divert when a load stands on it. AGV path, pickup, drop See \"Material flow: AGVs\". Times A time can be fixed or follow a distribution: uniform (least and most), exponential (mean: random arrivals), normal (mean and standard deviation, never below zero), triangular (least, most likely, most) or Erlang (mean and phases). All times are in seconds, lengths in meters."},{"slug":"flow-agv","scope":"addon:flow","title":"Material flow: AGVs","summary":"Lay out one-way or two-way AGV paths, a pickup and a drop, set the fleet, and read what the vehicles did.","section":"tasks","order":20,"keywords":["agv","automated guided vehicle","amr","path","pickup","drop","fleet","reservation","deadlock","zone control"],"headings":[{"id":"lay-out-the-paths","text":"Lay out the paths","level":2},{"id":"the-fleet","text":"The fleet","level":2},{"id":"how-the-vehicles-behave","text":"How the vehicles behave","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"lay-out-the-paths\"\u003ELay out the paths\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAGV paths are one-way segments, or two-way lanes when you tick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETwo-way lane\u003C/span\u003E. Add an \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAGV path\u003C/span\u003E joined to a pickup or to the end of another path; set its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETurn\u003C/span\u003E so it goes where you want. Segments whose ends touch (within 5 cm) form one network. Press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExample AGVs\u003C/span\u003E for a square loop with a pickup, a drop and two vehicles.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAGV pickup\u003C/span\u003E takes loads from a conveyor; its marker (beyond its front) must sit on a path end. An \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAGV drop\u003C/span\u003E puts loads onto a conveyor; its marker (behind it) must also sit on a path end. Set the pickup&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeliver to\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-fleet\"\u003EThe fleet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAGV fleet\u003C/span\u003E sets how many vehicles there are, their speed, and how long loading and unloading take. Vehicles start at the ends of segments spread around the network.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-the-vehicles-behave\"\u003EHow the vehicles behave\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA load waiting at a pickup calls the nearest free vehicle (by distance along the paths).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA vehicle drives on a one-way segment only when no other vehicle is on it: it waits at the end of its segment until the next one is clear. Two vehicles are never on one segment.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA free vehicle in another&#039;s way moves on to the next segment.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Etwo-way lane\u003C/strong\u003E is one reservation for both directions: only one vehicle at a time drives on it, either way. A vehicle is on the lane only while it drives along it; once it has arrived it stands at the node and the lane is free again (vehicles may pass each other at a node). A vehicle that turns back onto a lane another vehicle is waiting for hands it over first, so none is starved.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReserve junctions\u003C/span\u003E on, where three or more lanes meet only one vehicle at a time may be on the approach to the junction or standing at it, so paths never cross with two vehicles at the junction.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf vehicles end up waiting for each other in a circle, the run stops and says which vehicles and where. A free vehicle at the end of a lane with nowhere to move aside stops the run too (stuck). Add segments (a loop or a passing place) or use fewer vehicles.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EResults\u003C/span\u003E shows, for each vehicle, the loads delivered and how its time was spent: driving loaded, driving empty, waiting and idle.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECharging, vehicle sizes and traffic rules beyond lanes and junctions are not built yet.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Lay out the paths AGV paths are one-way segments, or two-way lanes when you tick Two-way lane. Add an AGV path joined to a pickup or to the end of another path; set its Length and Turn so it goes where you want. Segments whose ends touch (within 5 cm) form one network. Press Example AGVs for a square loop with a pickup, a drop and two vehicles. An AGV pickup takes loads from a conveyor; its marker (beyond its front) must sit on a path end. An AGV drop puts loads onto a conveyor; its marker (behind it) must also sit on a path end. Set the pickup's Deliver to. The fleet AGV fleet sets how many vehicles there are, their speed, and how long loading and unloading take. Vehicles start at the ends of segments spread around the network. How the vehicles behave A load waiting at a pickup calls the nearest free vehicle (by distance along the paths). A vehicle drives on a one-way segment only when no other vehicle is on it: it waits at the end of its segment until the next one is clear. Two vehicles are never on one segment. A free vehicle in another's way moves on to the next segment. A two-way lane is one reservation for both directions: only one vehicle at a time drives on it, either way. A vehicle is on the lane only while it drives along it; once it has arrived it stands at the node and the lane is free again (vehicles may pass each other at a node). A vehicle that turns back onto a lane another vehicle is waiting for hands it over first, so none is starved. With Reserve junctions on, where three or more lanes meet only one vehicle at a time may be on the approach to the junction or standing at it, so paths never cross with two vehicles at the junction. If vehicles end up waiting for each other in a circle, the run stops and says which vehicles and where. A free vehicle at the end of a lane with nowhere to move aside stops the run too (stuck). Add segments (a loop or a passing place) or use fewer vehicles. Results shows, for each vehicle, the loads delivered and how its time was spent: driving loaded, driving empty, waiting and idle. Charging, vehicle sizes and traffic rules beyond lanes and junctions are not built yet."},{"slug":"flow-robots","scope":"addon:flow","title":"Material flow: robot cells","summary":"Pick a robot model, check which stations it reaches, time a pick and place, and choose which program formats to save: a path list, ROS URDF, a joint trajectory, URScript.","section":"tasks","order":30,"keywords":["robot","robot arm","scara","delta","cartesian","gantry","kinematics","reach","cycle time","pick and place","offline programming","program","URDF","ROS","joint trajectory","URScript","robot formats","DH table"],"headings":[{"id":"add-a-robot-cell","text":"Add a robot cell","level":2},{"id":"check-the-reach","text":"Check the reach","level":2},{"id":"write-and-run-a-program","text":"Write and run a program","level":2},{"id":"pick-the-robot-model","text":"Pick the robot model","level":2},{"id":"choose-the-program-formats","text":"Choose the program formats","level":2},{"id":"what-is-not-supported","text":"What is not supported","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"add-a-robot-cell\"\u003EAdd a robot cell\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERobot cell\u003C/span\u003E section beside \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterial flow\u003C/span\u003E. Choose a robot, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd robot cell\u003C/span\u003E. Three starters are included: a 4-axis SCARA, a generic 6-axis articulated arm (about 0.85 m) and a delta picker. Set where the robot&#039;s base stands (x, y, z and turn) and the tool length.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"check-the-reach\"\u003ECheck the reach\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe list shows every station, buffer, source and sink in the layout and whether the robot&#039;s tool can reach the middle of its top, and the point 0.1 m above it. &quot;Not reachable&quot; says why: too far or too close, outside a joint or height limit, or the tool cannot point straight down there. The arm and SCARA are solved from their joint geometry; the delta from its published arm geometry.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"write-and-run-a-program\"\u003EWrite and run a program\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe program is plain text. The first line is \u003Ccode\u003ECCROBOT 1\u003C/code\u003E. Then:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Epoint NAME x y z\u003C/code\u003E names a point in metres (stations in the layout are already named by their id).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Espeed N\u003C/code\u003E sets the speed to N percent (1 to 100) of every limit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Emovej NAME\u003C/code\u003E moves all joints together to a point; \u003Ccode\u003Emovel NAME\u003C/code\u003E moves the tool in a straight line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Epick NAME\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Eplace NAME\u003C/code\u003E come down from 0.1 m above, grip or release for 0.3 s and go back up. Add \u003Ccode\u003Eclear 0.15\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003Egrip 0.5\u003C/code\u003E to change those.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ewait S\u003C/code\u003E waits S seconds; \u003Ccode\u003Ehome\u003C/code\u003E returns to the home pose.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESample program\u003C/span\u003E writes a pick at the first station and a place at the second. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun program\u003C/span\u003E gives each step&#039;s time and the cycle time. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse cycle time as station time\u003C/span\u003E sets a station&#039;s process time to that cycle, so the flow simulation uses it. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave program text…\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave path list (CSV)…\u003C/span\u003E write files.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pick-the-robot-model\"\u003EPick the robot model\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERobot model\u003C/span\u003E lists generic robots (6-axis, long-reach 6-axis, SCARA, long SCARA, delta, Cartesian gantry) and one with published figures: a 6-axis arm with the UR5e&#039;s printed Denavit-Hartenberg rows and datasheet ranges. Only numbers that two public pages print are used; no CAD and no controller data. Open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERobot joint table (editable)\u003C/span\u003E to change the rows (arms) or the lengths and ranges (SCARA, delta, Cartesian). Changes are saved with the document.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"choose-the-program-formats\"\u003EChoose the program formats\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProgram formats\u003C/span\u003E has one checkbox per format. The path list (CSV and JSON) is always saved. Tick any of the others, set the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGripper output number\u003C/span\u003E, and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave selected files…\u003C/span\u003E: the program is run and one file is saved per ticked format. Your choice is kept with the document and remembered as your default.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EROS URDF\u003C/strong\u003E is the robot itself (links, joints, limits from the table, the base placed in the layout). It needs a tree robot, so a delta is refused. Effort is a placeholder; there are no meshes or masses.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EROS joint trajectory\u003C/strong\u003E is the run as a JointTrajectory message in JSON: joint positions against time, straight moves sampled about every 5 cm. Signals are not part of that message.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUniversal Robots URScript\u003C/strong\u003E needs a 6-axis arm. It writes joint and straight moves with speeds, digital outputs, waits for inputs and sleeps. It does not set a tool or frame (set the tool centre point on the controller to the tool length shown at the top of the file) and does not blend.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery robot-language file starts with a comment saying it was generated by CadCraft, must be simulated and verified on your robot controller before it is run, and is not endorsed by the vendor. CadCraft has not run these programs on a robot. Joint values are those of the model&#039;s table: they are right for a real robot only if its zero positions and base frame match. A format refuses what it cannot say (a delta in URDF, a SCARA in URScript, a signal number it cannot address) and tells you why. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESources and formats left out\u003C/span\u003E lists the public pages each syntax was read from, with the date, and the languages that are not offered (ABB RAPID, KUKA KRL, Fanuc, Yaskawa, Kawasaki, Mitsubishi, Staubli, Denso) with the reason: a language is added only when two independent public sources confirm the statements it needs.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EExtra program words: \u003Ccode\u003Eout 3 on\u003C/code\u003E sets signal 3, \u003Ccode\u003Ewaitin 2 off\u003C/code\u003E waits for input 2.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-not-supported\"\u003EWhat is not supported\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERobot makers&#039; files are not read.\u003C/strong\u003E Only the formats listed above are written; no RAPID, KRL, TP or similar, and no program from another offline-programming product.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENo collision checking\u003C/strong\u003E, no tool or payload, no dynamics (a joint&#039;s speed and acceleration limits give the time). Times are a planning estimate, not a controller&#039;s number.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe 6-axis arm uses a numeric solver and the tool pointing straight down; other tool angles are not yet offered. The delta cannot do straight-line moves through its singular edges, and its home is the middle of its working height.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EVendor programs are minimal and not tested on a robot.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Add a robot cell Open the Robot cell section beside Material flow. Choose a robot, then Add robot cell. Three starters are included: a 4-axis SCARA, a generic 6-axis articulated arm (about 0.85 m) and a delta picker. Set where the robot's base stands (x, y, z and turn) and the tool length. Check the reach The list shows every station, buffer, source and sink in the layout and whether the robot's tool can reach the middle of its top, and the point 0.1 m above it. \"Not reachable\" says why: too far or too close, outside a joint or height limit, or the tool cannot point straight down there. The arm and SCARA are solved from their joint geometry; the delta from its published arm geometry. Write and run a program The program is plain text. The first line is CCROBOT 1. Then: point NAME x y z names a point in metres (stations in the layout are already named by their id). speed N sets the speed to N percent (1 to 100) of every limit. movej NAME moves all joints together to a point; movel NAME moves the tool in a straight line. pick NAME and place NAME come down from 0.1 m above, grip or release for 0.3 s and go back up. Add clear 0.15 or grip 0.5 to change those. wait S waits S seconds; home returns to the home pose. Sample program writes a pick at the first station and a place at the second. Run program gives each step's time and the cycle time. Use cycle time as station time sets a station's process time to that cycle, so the flow simulation uses it. Save program text… and Save path list (CSV)… write files. Pick the robot model Robot model lists generic robots (6-axis, long-reach 6-axis, SCARA, long SCARA, delta, Cartesian gantry) and one with published figures: a 6-axis arm with the UR5e's printed Denavit-Hartenberg rows and datasheet ranges. Only numbers that two public pages print are used; no CAD and no controller data. Open Robot joint table (editable) to change the rows (arms) or the lengths and ranges (SCARA, delta, Cartesian). Changes are saved with the document. Choose the program formats Program formats has one checkbox per format. The path list (CSV and JSON) is always saved. Tick any of the others, set the Gripper output number, and press Save selected files…: the program is run and one file is saved per ticked format. Your choice is kept with the document and remembered as your default. ROS URDF is the robot itself (links, joints, limits from the table, the base placed in the layout). It needs a tree robot, so a delta is refused. Effort is a placeholder; there are no meshes or masses. ROS joint trajectory is the run as a JointTrajectory message in JSON: joint positions against time, straight moves sampled about every 5 cm. Signals are not part of that message. Universal Robots URScript needs a 6-axis arm. It writes joint and straight moves with speeds, digital outputs, waits for inputs and sleeps. It does not set a tool or frame (set the tool centre point on the controller to the tool length shown at the top of the file) and does not blend. Every robot-language file starts with a comment saying it was generated by CadCraft, must be simulated and verified on your robot controller before it is run, and is not endorsed by the vendor. CadCraft has not run these programs on a robot. Joint values are those of the model's table: they are right for a real robot only if its zero positions and base frame match. A format refuses what it cannot say (a delta in URDF, a SCARA in URScript, a signal number it cannot address) and tells you why. Sources and formats left out lists the public pages each syntax was read from, with the date, and the languages that are not offered (ABB RAPID, KUKA KRL, Fanuc, Yaskawa, Kawasaki, Mitsubishi, Staubli, Denso) with the reason: a language is added only when two independent public sources confirm the statements it needs. Extra program words: out 3 on sets signal 3, waitin 2 off waits for input 2. What is not supported Robot makers' files are not read. Only the formats listed above are written; no RAPID, KRL, TP or similar, and no program from another offline-programming product. No collision checking, no tool or payload, no dynamics (a joint's speed and acceleration limits give the time). Times are a planning estimate, not a controller's number. The 6-axis arm uses a numeric solver and the tool pointing straight down; other tool angles are not yet offered. The delta cannot do straight-line moves through its singular edges, and its home is the middle of its working height. Vendor programs are minimal and not tested on a robot."},{"slug":"flow-shifts","scope":"addon:flow","title":"Material flow: shifts, breakdowns and live runs","summary":"Give stations and sources a weekly shift calendar, let stations and belts break down, and watch a run at the pace of the clock.","section":"tasks","order":30,"keywords":["shift","calendar","breakdown","mtbf","mttr","availability","live","real time","worker","cancel","progress"],"headings":[{"id":"shift-calendars","text":"Shift calendars","level":2},{"id":"breakdowns","text":"Breakdowns","level":2},{"id":"operator-pools-and-parking","text":"Operator pools and parking","level":2},{"id":"run-cancel-and-live","text":"Run, Cancel and Live","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"shift-calendars\"\u003EShift calendars\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShift calendars\u003C/span\u003E press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a shift calendar\u003C/span\u003E. A calendar is one week that repeats: windows of &quot;on&quot; time, each on some days from a start to an end time. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFill from\u003C/span\u003E offers common patterns (one, two or three shifts, every day). An end time earlier than the start runs past midnight (22:00 to 06:00 on Monday ends Tuesday 06:00). Time 0 of a run is \u003Cstrong\u003EMonday 00:00\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose the calendar in a station&#039;s or a source&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShift calendar\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Estation\u003C/strong\u003E starts no new work while its calendar is off. Work in progress finishes. Loads wait in its queue (up to the queue size) and the line backs up behind it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Esource\u003C/strong\u003E makes nothing while its calendar is off; the first load comes the moment it turns on again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EResults for such a station show how much of the run it was open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"breakdowns\"\u003EBreakdowns\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a station, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETime between breakdowns (0 = never)\u003C/span\u003E (hours of clock time, drawn from an exponential distribution) and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMean repair time\u003C/span\u003E (minutes, an Erlang-2 distribution) make it break down now and then. While it is broken nothing starts and work in progress pauses, then continues with the time it had left. Long-run availability is the time between breakdowns divided by that plus the repair time. Results show the share of time available and the number of breakdowns. Everything is seeded: the same seed gives the same breakdowns.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBelts (straight, curved and inclined conveyors) have the same two settings. A belt that breaks down stops: every load on it stays where it is, nothing gets on or off, and after the repair it runs on from where it stopped (playback shows it standing still). Gates break down too: a broken gate sticks shut, so loads wait in front of it until the repair. Vehicles have \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVehicle time between breakdowns (0 = never)\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVehicle mean repair time\u003C/span\u003E in the AGV fleet: a vehicle that is due to break down stops where it next stands (the end of the path it is on, or where it waits), keeps the lane it holds, and carries on with its job after the repair; results show each vehicle&#039;s availability and breakdowns. Zone conveyors do not break down yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"operator-pools-and-parking\"\u003EOperator pools and parking\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOperator pools\u003C/span\u003E press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd an operator pool\u003C/span\u003E, name it and say how many people it has. Give a station that pool in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOperator pool\u003C/span\u003E: each load it serves needs one person from the pool for the whole service, so with fewer people than servers some servers stand idle, and stations that share a pool take turns when a person comes free. Results show the pool&#039;s use. In the AGV fleet, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParking places\u003C/span\u003E lists AGV paths where idle vehicles with no job wait, one place each, instead of blocking a lane.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"run-cancel-and-live\"\u003ERun, Cancel and Live\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun\u003C/span\u003E simulates in a worker so the page stays usable. A line shows how much of the run is done; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E stops it. Where a browser cannot start a worker, the run happens on the page and the message says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELive\u003C/span\u003E runs the model at the pace of the clock: at 1× one model second takes one second (pick a faster speed to speed it up) and loads and vehicles move in the 3D view as it goes. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPause\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EResume\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop live\u003C/span\u003E are next to it. When the run reaches its end the results and playback are there as after a Run. The result is the same as a Run with the same seed.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Shift calendars Under Shift calendars press Add a shift calendar. A calendar is one week that repeats: windows of \"on\" time, each on some days from a start to an end time. Fill from offers common patterns (one, two or three shifts, every day). An end time earlier than the start runs past midnight (22:00 to 06:00 on Monday ends Tuesday 06:00). Time 0 of a run is Monday 00:00. Choose the calendar in a station's or a source's Shift calendar. A station starts no new work while its calendar is off. Work in progress finishes. Loads wait in its queue (up to the queue size) and the line backs up behind it. A source makes nothing while its calendar is off; the first load comes the moment it turns on again. Results for such a station show how much of the run it was open. Breakdowns On a station, Time between breakdowns (0 = never) (hours of clock time, drawn from an exponential distribution) and Mean repair time (minutes, an Erlang-2 distribution) make it break down now and then. While it is broken nothing starts and work in progress pauses, then continues with the time it had left. Long-run availability is the time between breakdowns divided by that plus the repair time. Results show the share of time available and the number of breakdowns. Everything is seeded: the same seed gives the same breakdowns. Belts (straight, curved and inclined conveyors) have the same two settings. A belt that breaks down stops: every load on it stays where it is, nothing gets on or off, and after the repair it runs on from where it stopped (playback shows it standing still). Gates break down too: a broken gate sticks shut, so loads wait in front of it until the repair. Vehicles have Vehicle time between breakdowns (0 = never) and Vehicle mean repair time in the AGV fleet: a vehicle that is due to break down stops where it next stands (the end of the path it is on, or where it waits), keeps the lane it holds, and carries on with its job after the repair; results show each vehicle's availability and breakdowns. Zone conveyors do not break down yet. Operator pools and parking Under Operator pools press Add an operator pool, name it and say how many people it has. Give a station that pool in Operator pool: each load it serves needs one person from the pool for the whole service, so with fewer people than servers some servers stand idle, and stations that share a pool take turns when a person comes free. Results show the pool's use. In the AGV fleet, Parking places lists AGV paths where idle vehicles with no job wait, one place each, instead of blocking a lane. Run, Cancel and Live Run simulates in a worker so the page stays usable. A line shows how much of the run is done; Cancel stops it. Where a browser cannot start a worker, the run happens on the page and the message says so. Live runs the model at the pace of the clock: at 1× one model second takes one second (pick a faster speed to speed it up) and loads and vehicles move in the 3D view as it goes. Pause, Resume and Stop live are next to it. When the run reaches its end the results and playback are there as after a Run. The result is the same as a Run with the same seed."},{"slug":"flow-orders","scope":"addon:flow","title":"Material flow: orders from a file","summary":"Bring arrivals in from a CSV file instead of random times, so the same file always gives the same run.","section":"tasks","order":35,"keywords":["orders","arrivals","csv","file","warehouse","schedule","time base","qty","repeatable"],"headings":[{"id":"the-file","text":"The file","level":2},{"id":"what-is-the-same-every-time","text":"What is the same every time","level":2},{"id":"a-bad-row-is-never-skipped","text":"A bad row is never skipped","level":2},{"id":"what-to-know","text":"What to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EUse this when you know when loads arrive (an order list, a delivery schedule, a day taken from a log) and do not want random times. Under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrders from a file\u003C/span\u003E press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen an orders file\u003C/span\u003E, then set a source&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMode\u003C/span\u003E to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrders from a file\u003C/span\u003E and pick the table in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrders table\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse the sample orders\u003C/span\u003E loads a small made-up file so you can see how it works.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-file\"\u003EThe file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA CSV file (commas, semicolons or tabs). The first row names the columns; the names are not case sensitive. Lines that are empty or start with # are not rows.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EColumn\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENeeded\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Etime_s, time_min, time_h or time\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhen the order arrives, counted from the start of the run, in seconds, minutes or hours. A column named just time uses the unit chosen under the file buttons (seconds at first).\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eone of these\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eclock\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA time of day (h:mm or h:mm:ss). The run starts at the first row&#039;s time of day, so 06:00:30 then 06:01:00 are 0 and 30 seconds.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eor this instead\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Etype\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe load&#039;s type name (letters, digits, _ and -). Empty means the source&#039;s first type.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eno\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Elength_m, length_mm or length\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe load&#039;s length (length is metres). Empty means the length the source gives that type, else 0.4 m.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eno\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eqty\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many loads arrive together, 1 to 1000. Empty means 1.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eno\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Esource\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe id of the source that takes the row. Empty means every source set to this table takes it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eno\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eorder, id or note\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA label for you. Kept, not used by the run.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eno\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOther columns are ignored.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-the-same-every-time\"\u003EWhat is the same every time\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERows are put in time order; rows at the same time keep their order in the file. A source in this mode draws no random numbers, so the same file gives the same run whatever the seed. Stations and breakdowns elsewhere in the layout still use the seed as before.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-bad-row-is-never-skipped\"\u003EA bad row is never skipped\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf any row cannot be read, nothing is loaded and the panel shows the first problem: the line number, the column and what the cell said. Fix that row and open the file again. Times may not be negative. A file may have up to 50,000 rows and a layout up to ten tables.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-to-know\"\u003EWhat to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOrders that come after the end of the run are not reached. The source&#039;s results list how many orders the table held.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA source&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShift calendar\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop after (0 = never)\u003C/span\u003E do not apply to orders: the file is the schedule.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA load longer than the belt it must ride is reported as a problem before the run.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe tables are saved in the ModelCraft file with the layout. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as CSV\u003C/span\u003E writes a table back out in the columns above (time in seconds).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThis is a way to feed the model what you already know. It does not read any warehouse system&#039;s own format.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Use this when you know when loads arrive (an order list, a delivery schedule, a day taken from a log) and do not want random times. Under Orders from a file press Open an orders file, then set a source's Mode to Orders from a file and pick the table in Orders table. Use the sample orders loads a small made-up file so you can see how it works. The file A CSV file (commas, semicolons or tabs). The first row names the columns; the names are not case sensitive. Lines that are empty or start with # are not rows. Column Meaning Needed time_s, time_min, time_h or time When the order arrives, counted from the start of the run, in seconds, minutes or hours. A column named just time uses the unit chosen under the file buttons (seconds at first). one of these clock A time of day (h:mm or h:mm:ss). The run starts at the first row's time of day, so 06:00:30 then 06:01:00 are 0 and 30 seconds. or this instead type The load's type name (letters, digits, _ and -). Empty means the source's first type. no length_m, length_mm or length The load's length (length is metres). Empty means the length the source gives that type, else 0.4 m. no qty How many loads arrive together, 1 to 1000. Empty means 1. no source The id of the source that takes the row. Empty means every source set to this table takes it. no order, id or note A label for you. Kept, not used by the run. no Other columns are ignored. What is the same every time Rows are put in time order; rows at the same time keep their order in the file. A source in this mode draws no random numbers, so the same file gives the same run whatever the seed. Stations and breakdowns elsewhere in the layout still use the seed as before. A bad row is never skipped If any row cannot be read, nothing is loaded and the panel shows the first problem: the line number, the column and what the cell said. Fix that row and open the file again. Times may not be negative. A file may have up to 50,000 rows and a layout up to ten tables. What to know Orders that come after the end of the run are not reached. The source's results list how many orders the table held. A source's Shift calendar and Stop after (0 = never) do not apply to orders: the file is the schedule. A load longer than the belt it must ride is reported as a problem before the run. The tables are saved in the ModelCraft file with the layout. Save as CSV writes a table back out in the columns above (time in seconds). This is a way to feed the model what you already know. It does not read any warehouse system's own format."},{"slug":"flow-plc-mode","scope":"addon:flow","title":"Material flow: PLC mode, Soft or Real","summary":"Run a soft PLC on the model with no hardware, or connect a real PLC over Modbus TCP through Studio, and map the model's tags to its addresses.","section":"tasks","order":40,"keywords":["plc","soft plc","real plc","modbus","modbus tcp","emulation","controls","address map","registers","coils","structured text","ladder","openplc","modpoll"],"headings":[{"id":"choose-the-mode","text":"Choose the mode","level":2},{"id":"soft-a-soft-plc-on-the-model","text":"Soft: a soft PLC on the model","level":2},{"id":"real-a-plc-over-modbus-tcp","text":"Real: a PLC over Modbus TCP","level":2},{"id":"checking-it-with-an-external-modbus-server","text":"Checking it with an external Modbus server","level":2},{"id":"the-other-direction","text":"The other direction","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESimulation only.\u003C/strong\u003E Never drive real machinery from this emulation unattended, and never rely on it for safety. An emergency stop here is a test input. You are responsible for what a connected PLC does.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"choose-the-mode\"\u003EChoose the mode\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPLC mode\u003C/span\u003E section beside \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterial flow\u003C/span\u003E and pick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESoft (no hardware)\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReal (a PLC over Modbus TCP)\u003C/span\u003E. The choice is saved with the model. Both modes use the same tags: every photo-eye gives \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;eye&gt;_covered\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;eye&gt;_standing\u003C/code\u003E, every stop gate in PLC mode gives \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;gate&gt;_is_open\u003C/code\u003E (to the PLC) and \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;gate&gt;_open\u003C/code\u003E (the PLC&#039;s command), and the model gives \u003Ccode\u003Eestop_ok\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eloads_done\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Eloads_inside\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"soft-a-soft-plc-on-the-model\"\u003ESoft: a soft PLC on the model\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe same soft PLC the Digital twin add-on uses runs your program on the model, in this window or in Studio. Write Structured Text (IEC 61131-3) or paste PLCopen XML. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStarter program\u003C/span\u003E writes a first rule for every gate. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun 10 minutes (fast)\u003C/span\u003E runs the model faster than real time and shows loads done, loads an hour and the scans; the same program and seed give the same result every time. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAttach to the live run\u003C/span\u003E makes the soft PLC drive the gates of a Live run, scanning with the model&#039;s clock.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESoft mode works in a browser tab and in Studio. It needs the Digital twin files that ship with ModelCraft; the add-on itself does not have to be switched on.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"real-a-plc-over-modbus-tcp\"\u003EReal: a PLC over Modbus TCP\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe model plays the plant and a real PLC plays the controller. Each cycle the model&#039;s sensors are written to the PLC (coils and holding registers) and the PLC&#039;s output coils are read back and applied to the gates. Studio opens the connection, so Real mode is only available in CadCraft Studio. In a browser tab it is switched off and says why: a browser cannot open a raw TCP connection. A Studio older than 0.9 has no PLC client and says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPLC address (IPv4)\u003C/span\u003E and port. Studio accepts numeric addresses on this computer or a private network (10.x, 172.16 to 31.x, 192.168.x, 169.254.x), nothing else. The address stays on this computer and is never saved in the model file. Modbus TCP has no password.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECheck the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAddress map\u003C/span\u003E: one row per tag with its type, table (coil, discrete input, holding register, input register), address and scale. Tags are written to coils or holding registers; PLC outputs can be read from any table. A scale multiplies the model&#039;s value on the way out and divides on the way in. Neighbouring addresses are sent as one request. The defaults put the bits on coils 0 upward, the counters on holding registers 0 and 1, and the gate commands on coils 100 upward. Edit them to match your PLC program.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart a Live run, tick the box that you understand the safety line, and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConnect to the PLC\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the PLC stops answering, or five exchanges in a row fail, the link is closed and every gate is closed. Only function codes 1 to 6, 15 and 16 are sent. The model is kinematic, so a PLC cannot make a belt run faster.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"checking-it-with-an-external-modbus-server\"\u003EChecking it with an external Modbus server\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe suite tests Real mode against a small Modbus TCP server written for the tests. To try it yourself without a PLC, run any Modbus TCP server on your own computer (for example OpenPLC or a simulator) and read and write it with a tool such as modpoll. These are separate programs you run yourself; CadCraft does not include or link them. Nothing here has been run against real PLC hardware yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-other-direction\"\u003EThe other direction\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf your PLC is the Modbus client and polls the model, use the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPLC (Modbus TCP)\u003C/span\u003E section above instead: Studio listens on this computer and the page answers with the fixed address list.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Simulation only. Never drive real machinery from this emulation unattended, and never rely on it for safety. An emergency stop here is a test input. You are responsible for what a connected PLC does. Choose the mode Open the PLC mode section beside Material flow and pick Soft (no hardware) or Real (a PLC over Modbus TCP). The choice is saved with the model. Both modes use the same tags: every photo-eye gives \u003Ceye\u003E_covered and \u003Ceye\u003E_standing, every stop gate in PLC mode gives \u003Cgate\u003E_is_open (to the PLC) and \u003Cgate\u003E_open (the PLC's command), and the model gives estop_ok, loads_done and loads_inside. Soft: a soft PLC on the model The same soft PLC the Digital twin add-on uses runs your program on the model, in this window or in Studio. Write Structured Text (IEC 61131-3) or paste PLCopen XML. Starter program writes a first rule for every gate. Run 10 minutes (fast) runs the model faster than real time and shows loads done, loads an hour and the scans; the same program and seed give the same result every time. Attach to the live run makes the soft PLC drive the gates of a Live run, scanning with the model's clock. Soft mode works in a browser tab and in Studio. It needs the Digital twin files that ship with ModelCraft; the add-on itself does not have to be switched on. Real: a PLC over Modbus TCP The model plays the plant and a real PLC plays the controller. Each cycle the model's sensors are written to the PLC (coils and holding registers) and the PLC's output coils are read back and applied to the gates. Studio opens the connection, so Real mode is only available in CadCraft Studio. In a browser tab it is switched off and says why: a browser cannot open a raw TCP connection. A Studio older than 0.9 has no PLC client and says so. Set the PLC address (IPv4) and port. Studio accepts numeric addresses on this computer or a private network (10.x, 172.16 to 31.x, 192.168.x, 169.254.x), nothing else. The address stays on this computer and is never saved in the model file. Modbus TCP has no password. Check the Address map: one row per tag with its type, table (coil, discrete input, holding register, input register), address and scale. Tags are written to coils or holding registers; PLC outputs can be read from any table. A scale multiplies the model's value on the way out and divides on the way in. Neighbouring addresses are sent as one request. The defaults put the bits on coils 0 upward, the counters on holding registers 0 and 1, and the gate commands on coils 100 upward. Edit them to match your PLC program. Start a Live run, tick the box that you understand the safety line, and press Connect to the PLC. If the PLC stops answering, or five exchanges in a row fail, the link is closed and every gate is closed. Only function codes 1 to 6, 15 and 16 are sent. The model is kinematic, so a PLC cannot make a belt run faster. Checking it with an external Modbus server The suite tests Real mode against a small Modbus TCP server written for the tests. To try it yourself without a PLC, run any Modbus TCP server on your own computer (for example OpenPLC or a simulator) and read and write it with a tool such as modpoll. These are separate programs you run yourself; CadCraft does not include or link them. Nothing here has been run against real PLC hardware yet. The other direction If your PLC is the Modbus client and polls the model, use the PLC (Modbus TCP) section above instead: Studio listens on this computer and the page answers with the fixed address list."},{"slug":"flow-results","scope":"addon:flow","title":"Material flow: results and how the simulation works","summary":"What each result means, how warm-up and the random seed work, the CSV, and the known answers the simulation is tested against.","section":"reference","order":30,"keywords":["results","statistics","throughput","lead time","utilization","queue","warm-up","seed","csv","discrete event","queueing theory"],"headings":[{"id":"results","text":"Results","level":2},{"id":"warm-up-and-the-seed","text":"Warm-up and the seed","level":2},{"id":"playback","text":"Playback","level":2},{"id":"csv","text":"CSV","level":2},{"id":"tested-against-known-answers","text":"Tested against known answers","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"results\"\u003EResults\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EResult\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeans\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELoads finished, per hour\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELoads that reached a sink after the warm-up.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELead time\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFrom a load being made at its source to leaving at a sink: mean, 95th percentile (95 in 100 took less) and the longest.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIn the system at the end\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELoads still on belts, in queues or on vehicles when the run ends.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUtilization\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe share of time a station&#039;s servers were working. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlocked\u003C/span\u003E: done, but the way on was full.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EQueue, wait in queue\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELoads waiting in front of a station, averaged over time, and how long each waited.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELoads on it, end blocked\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFor a belt: how many loads were on it, and the share of time its end was held.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"warm-up-and-the-seed\"\u003EWarm-up and the seed\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA run starts empty. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWarm-up\u003C/span\u003E minutes are simulated but not counted, so the results describe the system once it is full. Random times use the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERandom seed\u003C/span\u003E: the same layout and seed give exactly the same results on every computer; another seed gives another, equally likely day. Run a few seeds before trusting a small difference.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach item draws its own random numbers, so adding an item elsewhere does not change what the others draw.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"playback\"\u003EPlayback\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe first two hours of a run are kept for playback; the results cover the whole run. On a belt a load is drawn where the simulation put it; in a queue, in a row before the station.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"csv\"\u003ECSV\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave results as CSV\u003C/span\u003E writes one row per element and measure, with full precision: the run (seed, hours, totals, lead times), each load type, each element and each vehicle.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tested-against-known-answers\"\u003ETested against known answers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe simulation is checked against queueing theory and simple cases worked out by hand, among them: a station with random arrivals and a fixed time at 80 % load (mean wait 16 s for an 8 s job, within 4 %), the same with random times on one and two servers, a belt&#039;s travel time and capacity (a 10 m belt with 0.4 m loads 0.1 m apart holds 20), loads leaving a released queue one spacing apart, merge priority, and every divert rule. Shifts and breakdowns are checked the same way: a station open one hour of two completes exactly the loads that fit in that hour, and a saturated station that breaks down follows availability = time between breakdowns ÷ (that + repair time) within 3 %.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Results Result Means Loads finished, per hour Loads that reached a sink after the warm-up. Lead time From a load being made at its source to leaving at a sink: mean, 95th percentile (95 in 100 took less) and the longest. In the system at the end Loads still on belts, in queues or on vehicles when the run ends. Utilization The share of time a station's servers were working. Blocked: done, but the way on was full. Queue, wait in queue Loads waiting in front of a station, averaged over time, and how long each waited. Loads on it, end blocked For a belt: how many loads were on it, and the share of time its end was held. Warm-up and the seed A run starts empty. Warm-up minutes are simulated but not counted, so the results describe the system once it is full. Random times use the Random seed: the same layout and seed give exactly the same results on every computer; another seed gives another, equally likely day. Run a few seeds before trusting a small difference. Each item draws its own random numbers, so adding an item elsewhere does not change what the others draw. Playback The first two hours of a run are kept for playback; the results cover the whole run. On a belt a load is drawn where the simulation put it; in a queue, in a row before the station. CSV Save results as CSV writes one row per element and measure, with full precision: the run (seed, hours, totals, lead times), each load type, each element and each vehicle. Tested against known answers The simulation is checked against queueing theory and simple cases worked out by hand, among them: a station with random arrivals and a fixed time at 80 % load (mean wait 16 s for an 8 s job, within 4 %), the same with random times on one and two servers, a belt's travel time and capacity (a 10 m belt with 0.4 m loads 0.1 m apart holds 20), loads leaving a released queue one spacing apart, merge priority, and every divert rule. Shifts and breakdowns are checked the same way: a station open one hour of two completes exactly the loads that fit in that hour, and a saturated station that breaks down follows availability = time between breakdowns ÷ (that + repair time) within 3 %."},{"slug":"flow-limits","scope":"addon:flow","title":"Material flow: limits and what is not built yet","summary":"What the Material flow add-on does not do yet, and the limits of this version.","section":"trouble","order":90,"keywords":["limits","not built","physics","plc","emulation","modbus","opc ua","robot","crane","worker","live"],"headings":[{"id":"limits-of-this-version","text":"Limits of this version","level":2},{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"limits-of-this-version\"\u003ELimits of this version\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENo physics.\u003C/strong\u003E Loads are moved by rules; they never tip, collide or fall. A belt moves loads at its speed; acceleration is not modeled.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBelts are joined by touching ports.\u003C/strong\u003E The layout is saved in the \u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E file; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave…\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen…\u003C/span\u003E also keep it as a separate file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAGVs:\u003C/strong\u003E one-way segments and two-way lanes with junction reservation; no battery charging or vehicle sizes yet; parking is one place per vehicle on a path you name. Head-on traffic in a single corridor with no way round stops the run and says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBreakdowns are for stations, belts, gates and vehicles\u003C/strong\u003E (a time between breakdowns and a mean repair time); zone conveyors do not break down yet. A vehicle&#039;s breakdown takes effect where it next stands, not in the middle of a path. Operator pools serve stations only (one person per service; no skills, breaks or walking time).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA run goes to a worker, with a progress line and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E; where a browser cannot start a worker it runs on the page and says so. Very long runs stop at an event limit and say so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELive\u003C/strong\u003E keeps the first 200 000 load positions of a run for the 3D view; after that, loads stop being drawn (the numbers go on).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EConnecting a PLC to the model (emulation): sensors as PLC inputs, motors and diverts as PLC outputs. Planned for CadCraft Studio, which can talk to a PLC; the web edition will keep simulating only.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERobots, cranes, shuttles and lifts; pallet and tote stacks; walking time and skills for operators.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERobots:\u003C/strong\u003E a robot cell checks reach and cycle time and writes a path list, ROS URDF, a joint trajectory and URScript; no collision checking, and no other maker language yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Limits of this version No physics. Loads are moved by rules; they never tip, collide or fall. A belt moves loads at its speed; acceleration is not modeled. Belts are joined by touching ports. The layout is saved in the .modelcraft file; Save… and Open… also keep it as a separate file. AGVs: one-way segments and two-way lanes with junction reservation; no battery charging or vehicle sizes yet; parking is one place per vehicle on a path you name. Head-on traffic in a single corridor with no way round stops the run and says so. Breakdowns are for stations, belts, gates and vehicles (a time between breakdowns and a mean repair time); zone conveyors do not break down yet. A vehicle's breakdown takes effect where it next stands, not in the middle of a path. Operator pools serve stations only (one person per service; no skills, breaks or walking time). A run goes to a worker, with a progress line and Cancel; where a browser cannot start a worker it runs on the page and says so. Very long runs stop at an event limit and say so. Live keeps the first 200 000 load positions of a run for the 3D view; after that, loads stop being drawn (the numbers go on). Not built yet Connecting a PLC to the model (emulation): sensors as PLC inputs, motors and diverts as PLC outputs. Planned for CadCraft Studio, which can talk to a PLC; the web edition will keep simulating only. Robots, cranes, shuttles and lifts; pallet and tote stacks; walking time and skills for operators. Robots: a robot cell checks reach and cycle time and writes a path list, ROS URDF, a joint trajectory and URScript; no collision checking, and no other maker language yet."}]};
